{"assertions":[{"answerability":"answerable","basis":"Prat's article \"St. Paul\" in the same encyclopedia (vol. 11, 1911) gives a single year for the four captivity letters: \"(Philemon; Colossians; Ephesians; Philippians), 61\" — a year that falls outside the Philippians article's own 62-64 window.","basis_class":"traditional-catholic","date":"61 A.D.","derived":false,"disposition":"disputed","inherited":true,"label":"(Philemon; Colossians; Ephesians; Philippians), 61","note":"","precision":"year","profile":"catholic-traditional-v1","relation":"composition","reporting_exception":null,"scope":"Phil","sources":["artifact.catholic-encyclopedia.volume-11.new-york-1911.newadvent-11567b-bff0dda8"],"subject":"composition.epistle-to-the-philippians","title":"The Epistle to the Philippians"},{"answerability":"answerable","basis":"Vander Heeren's article \"Epistle to the Philippians\" in the Catholic Encyclopedia (vol. 12, 1911): \"it is certain that it was written not at Caesarea, as some have maintained, but at Rome (A.D. 62-64).\"","basis_class":"traditional-catholic","date":"62 A.D. to 64 A.D.","derived":false,"disposition":"disputed","inherited":true,"label":"at Rome (A.D. 62-64)","note":"Within that window the article leaves the position unsettled: \"Critics do not agree as to whether the Epistle was written at the beginning of the sojourn at Rome or at the end, before or after the other three Epistles of the captivity\", the writer himself holding for the end.","precision":"interval","profile":"catholic-traditional-v1","relation":"composition","reporting_exception":null,"scope":"Phil","sources":["artifact.catholic-encyclopedia.volume-12.new-york-1911.newadvent-12008a-1e39e824"],"subject":"composition.epistle-to-the-philippians","title":"The Epistle to the Philippians"}],"chronology_status":"composition-only","locus":"Phil.1.20","reason":"","requested_profile":"catholic-comprehensive-v1","resolved_profiles":{"composition":"catholic-traditional-v1"},"status":"ok","v":1